Based on local repository evidence (no internet access was available in this sandbox — network tool calls were denied), here's what I found and my decision.

**Key findings:**
- `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-data-couchbase-test:4.1.1` is the class-bearing "test-slice support" module (home of `@DataCouchbaseTest` and its supporting autoconfiguration/exclude-filter classes) — distinct from the empty `spring-boot-starter-*-test` POM aggregators already in this repo's metadata (`spring-boot-starter-couchbase-test`, `spring-boot-starter-data-couchbase-test`), both of which are marked `not-for-native-image` because their jars contain no class files.
- The sibling module `org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-data-couchbase:4.1.1` already has a working test (`tests/src/org.springframework.boot/spring-boot-data-couchbase/4.1.1`) that exercises `DataCouchbaseAutoConfiguration` purely through `ApplicationContextRunner` + `ImportCandidates` reflection — no live Couchbase connection, no Docker. That's because `DataCouchbaseAutoConfiguration` only wires the Spring Data mapping/conversion layer; the actual `Cluster`/connection bean lives in the separate `spring-boot-couchbase` autoconfiguration, which that test deliberately excludes.
- This repo has never pinned a Couchbase Docker image (`tests/tck-build-logic/.../allowed-docker-images` has none), and the only existing `com.couchbase.client` tests cover micrometer metrics/tracing instrumentation, not a live cluster.
- The same separation-of-concerns likely applies to the `-test` slice module: its own classes (the `@DataCouchbaseTest` annotation, type-exclude filter, test auto-configuration) can plausibly be exercised the same reflection/`ApplicationContextRunner` way as the sibling, without booting a connected cluster — that only becomes necessary if a generated test tries to run the *full* `@DataCouchbaseTest` slice end-to-end against a real bucket.

Given no concrete evidence of required extra setup, and strong local precedent that this library family avoids live infrastructure, I'm returning `no_action`, with guidance to steer the test toward the proven pattern and a flagged risk in case verification proves otherwise.

```json
